WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.875 --> 00:00:18.889
I've been a political cartoonist
on the global stage for the last 20 years.

00:00:19.543 --> 00:00:23.212
Hey, we have seen a lot of things
happen in those 20 years.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:27.020
We saw three different Catholic popes,

00:00:27.044 --> 00:00:31.346
and we witnessed that unique moment:

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:34.000
the election of a pope
on St. Peter's Square --

00:00:34.024 --> 00:00:36.320
you know, the little white smoke

00:00:37.571 --> 00:00:39.641
and the official announcement.

00:00:39.665 --> 00:00:41.022
[It's a boy!]

00:00:41.046 --> 00:00:43.232
(Laughter)

00:00:43.256 --> 00:00:47.208
(Applause)

00:00:49.266 --> 00:00:52.765
We saw four American presidents.

00:00:52.789 --> 00:00:54.120
Obama, of course.

00:00:54.144 --> 00:00:56.558
Oh, Europeans liked him a lot.

00:00:56.582 --> 00:00:58.877
He was a multilateralist.

00:00:58.901 --> 00:01:00.143
He favored diplomacy.

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:02.527
He wanted to be friends with Iran.

00:01:03.187 --> 00:01:07.815
(Laughter)

00:01:09.831 --> 00:01:10.989
And then ...

00:01:12.181 --> 00:01:14.416
reality imitated caricature

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:19.459
the day Donald Trump became the President
of the United States of America.

00:01:19.483 --> 00:01:22.326
(Laughter)

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.867
(Applause)

00:01:27.608 --> 00:01:30.092
You know, people come to us and they say,

00:01:30.116 --> 00:01:35.328
"It's too easy for you cartoonists.
I mean -- with people like Trump?"

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:38.337
Well, no, it's not easy

00:01:38.361 --> 00:01:41.869
to caricature a man
who is himself a caricature.

00:01:41.893 --> 00:01:43.226
(Laughter)

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:44.419
No.

00:01:44.443 --> 00:01:48.222
(Applause)

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:51.732
Populists are no easy target for satire

00:01:51.756 --> 00:01:54.280
because you try to nail them down one day,

00:01:54.304 --> 00:01:55.921
and the next day, they outdo you.

00:01:55.945 --> 00:01:59.331
For example, as soon as he was elected,

00:01:59.355 --> 00:02:03.495
I tried to imagine the tweet
that Trump would send on Christmas Eve.

00:02:03.519 --> 00:02:05.599
So I did this, OK?

00:02:05.623 --> 00:02:08.790
[Merry Christmas to all!
Except all those pathetic losers. So sad.]

00:02:08.814 --> 00:02:10.600
(Laughter)

00:02:10.624 --> 00:02:13.204
And basically, the next day,
Trump tweeted this:

00:02:13.228 --> 00:02:15.696
[Happy New Year to all,
including to my many enemies

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.874
and those who have fought me
and lost so badly

00:02:17.898 --> 00:02:19.769
they just don't know what to do. Love!]

00:02:19.793 --> 00:02:20.975
(Laughter)

00:02:20.999 --> 00:02:22.443
It's the same!

00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:25.047
(Applause)

00:02:25.071 --> 00:02:27.535
This is the era of strongmen.

00:02:27.559 --> 00:02:32.935
And soon, Donald Trump was able to meet
his personal hero, Vladimir Putin,

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:35.755
and this is how the first meeting went:

00:02:35.779 --> 00:02:38.474
[I'll help you find the hackers.
Give me your password.]

00:02:38.498 --> 00:02:41.244
(Laughter)

00:02:41.268 --> 00:02:43.679
And I'm not inventing anything.

00:02:44.427 --> 00:02:48.464
He came out of that first meeting
saying that the two of them had agreed

00:02:48.488 --> 00:02:50.880
on a joint task force on cybersecurity.

00:02:50.904 --> 00:02:53.240
This is true, if you do remember.

00:02:53.264 --> 00:02:57.922
Oh, who would have imagined
the things we saw over these 20 years.

00:02:57.946 --> 00:03:01.547
We saw Great Britain run towards
a European Union exit.

00:03:01.571 --> 00:03:02.742
[Hard Brexit?]

00:03:02.766 --> 00:03:05.859
(Laughter)

00:03:08.369 --> 00:03:10.847
In the Middle East,
we believed for a while

00:03:10.871 --> 00:03:14.056
in the democratic miracle
of the Arab Spring.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.473
We saw dictators fall,
we saw others hang on.

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:20.589
(Laughter)

00:03:21.107 --> 00:03:25.014
And then there is the timeless
Kim dynasty of North Korea.

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:28.369
These guys seem to be coming
straight out of Cartoon Network.

00:03:28.393 --> 00:03:31.488
I was blessed to be able
to draw two of them.

00:03:31.512 --> 00:03:33.265
Kim Jong-il, the father,

00:03:33.289 --> 00:03:36.607
when he died a few years ago,
that was a very dangerous moment.

00:03:36.631 --> 00:03:37.824
[That was close!]

00:03:37.848 --> 00:03:40.471
(Laughter)

00:03:40.495 --> 00:03:41.699
That was --

00:03:41.723 --> 00:03:44.643
(Applause)

00:03:46.977 --> 00:03:49.647
And then the son, Kim Jong-un,

00:03:49.671 --> 00:03:52.352
proved himself a worthy
successor to the throne.

00:03:52.376 --> 00:03:54.926
He's now friends with the US president.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.503
They meet each other all the time,
and they talk like friends.

00:03:59.527 --> 00:04:01.065
[What kind of hair gel?]

00:04:01.089 --> 00:04:04.489
(Laughter)

00:04:06.138 --> 00:04:08.378
Should we be surprised

00:04:08.402 --> 00:04:10.185
to be living in a world

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:12.254
ruled by egomaniacs?

00:04:12.796 --> 00:04:15.535
What if they were just
a reflection of ourselves?

00:04:16.081 --> 00:04:17.971
I mean, look at us, each of us.

00:04:19.004 --> 00:04:20.931
(Laughter)

00:04:21.737 --> 00:04:25.300
Yeah, we love our smartphones;

00:04:25.324 --> 00:04:27.424
we love our selfies;

00:04:27.448 --> 00:04:29.057
we love ourselves.

00:04:29.081 --> 00:04:32.927
And thanks to Facebook,
we have a lot of friends

00:04:32.951 --> 00:04:34.812
all over the world.

00:04:34.836 --> 00:04:37.113
Mark Zuckerberg is our friend.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:40.500
(Laughter)

00:04:42.571 --> 00:04:45.837
You know, he and his peers
in Silicon Valley

00:04:45.861 --> 00:04:48.806
are the kings and the emperors

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.185
of our time.

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:55.596
Showing that the emperors have no clothes,

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.885
that's the task of satire, right?

00:04:57.909 --> 00:04:59.240
Speaking truth to power.

00:04:59.264 --> 00:05:03.524
This has always been the historical role
of political cartooning.

00:05:03.548 --> 00:05:08.485
In the 1830s, postrevolutionary France
under King Louis Philippe,

00:05:08.509 --> 00:05:13.152
journalists and caricaturists fought hard
for the freedom of the press.

00:05:13.725 --> 00:05:17.191
They were jailed, they were fined,
but they prevailed.

00:05:17.215 --> 00:05:21.083
And this caricature of the king by Daumier

00:05:21.107 --> 00:05:23.122
came to define the monarch.

00:05:23.146 --> 00:05:24.558
It marked history.

00:05:24.582 --> 00:05:31.144
It became the timeless symbol
of satire triumphing over autocracy.

00:05:31.168 --> 00:05:33.839
Today, 200 years after Daumier,

00:05:33.863 --> 00:05:38.261
are political cartoons
at risk of disappearing?

00:05:39.942 --> 00:05:45.731
Take this blank space on the front page of
Turkish opposition newspaper "Cumhuriyet."

00:05:45.755 --> 00:05:49.204
This is where Musa Kart's
cartoon used to appear.

00:05:49.228 --> 00:05:54.321
In 2018, Musa Kart was sentenced
to three years in jail.

00:05:54.345 --> 00:05:55.651
For doing what?

00:05:55.675 --> 00:05:59.693
For doing political cartoons
in Erdoğan's Turkey.

00:06:00.305 --> 00:06:05.822
Cartoonists from Venezuela, Russia, Syria
have been forced into exile.

00:06:06.961 --> 00:06:08.442
Look at this image.

00:06:09.744 --> 00:06:11.984
It seems so innocent, right?

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.900
Yet it is so provocative.

00:06:15.391 --> 00:06:16.936
When he posted this image,

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.213
Hani Abbas knew it would change his life.

00:06:20.773 --> 00:06:25.261
It was in 2012, and the Syrians
were taking to the streets.

00:06:25.285 --> 00:06:30.301
Of course, the little red flower
is the symbol of the Syrian revolution.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:33.534
So pretty soon, the regime was after him,

00:06:33.558 --> 00:06:35.186
and he had to flee the country.

00:06:35.805 --> 00:06:39.092
A good friend of his,
cartoonist Akram Raslan,

00:06:39.116 --> 00:06:40.899
didn't make it out of Syria.

00:06:41.331 --> 00:06:43.707
He died under torture.

00:06:47.345 --> 00:06:50.816
In the United States of America recently,

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:56.492
some of the very top cartoonists,
like Nick Anderson and Rob Rogers --

00:06:56.516 --> 00:06:57.811
this is a cartoon by Rob --

00:06:57.835 --> 00:07:00.787
[Memorial Day 2018.
(on tombstone) Truth. Honor. Rule of Law.]

00:07:00.811 --> 00:07:02.010
they lost their positions

00:07:02.034 --> 00:07:05.135
because their publishers
found their work too critical of Trump.

00:07:05.558 --> 00:07:09.232
And the same happened
to Canadian cartoonist Michael de Adder.

00:07:09.799 --> 00:07:11.725
Hey, maybe we should start worrying.

00:07:12.506 --> 00:07:16.003
Political cartoons
were born with democracy,

00:07:16.027 --> 00:07:18.496
and they are challenged when freedom is.

00:07:19.262 --> 00:07:20.953
You know, over the years,

00:07:20.977 --> 00:07:25.772
with the Cartooning for Peace Foundation
and other initiatives,

00:07:25.796 --> 00:07:28.896
Kofi Annan -- this is not well
known -- he was the honorary chair

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.079
of our foundation,

00:07:30.103 --> 00:07:32.802
the late Kofi Annan, Nobel Peace Laureate.

00:07:32.826 --> 00:07:35.172
He was a great defender of cartoons.

00:07:35.196 --> 00:07:40.163
Or, on the board of the Association
of American Editorial Cartoonists,

00:07:40.187 --> 00:07:45.860
we have advocated on behalf of jailed,
threatened, fired, exiled cartoonists.

00:07:46.533 --> 00:07:50.515
But I never saw a case
of someone losing his job

00:07:50.539 --> 00:07:52.468
over a cartoon he didn't do.

00:07:53.564 --> 00:07:54.988
Well, that happened to me.

00:07:57.456 --> 00:08:01.047
For the last 20 years, I have been
with the "International Herald Tribune"

00:08:01.071 --> 00:08:02.321
and the "New York Times."

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:04.186
Then something happened.

00:08:04.768 --> 00:08:07.516
In April 2019,

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:10.842
a cartoon by a famous
Portuguese cartoonist,

00:08:10.866 --> 00:08:15.006
which was first published
in a newspaper "El Expresso" in Lisbon,

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:17.260
was picked by an editor
at the "New York Times"

00:08:17.284 --> 00:08:20.022
and reprinted in
the international editions.

00:08:21.609 --> 00:08:22.974
This thing blew up.

00:08:23.343 --> 00:08:26.445
It was denounced as anti-Semitic,

00:08:26.469 --> 00:08:28.715
triggered widespread outrage,

00:08:28.739 --> 00:08:29.906
apologies

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:32.626
and a lot of damage control by the Times.

00:08:33.133 --> 00:08:35.962
A month after, my editor told me

00:08:35.986 --> 00:08:39.506
they were ending
political cartoons altogether.

00:08:41.069 --> 00:08:45.115
So we could, and we should,
have a discussion about that cartoon.

00:08:45.764 --> 00:08:49.921
Some people say it reminds them
of the worst anti-Semitic propaganda.

00:08:49.945 --> 00:08:51.639
Others, including in Israel,

00:08:51.663 --> 00:08:54.526
say no, it's just
a harsh criticism of Trump,

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:58.195
who is shown as blindly following
the Prime Minister of Israel.

00:08:58.934 --> 00:09:00.744
I have some issues with this cartoon,

00:09:00.768 --> 00:09:03.616
but that discussion did not happen
at the "New York Times."

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:06.520
Under attack, they took the easiest path:

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:11.471
in order to not have problems
with political cartoons in the future,

00:09:11.495 --> 00:09:13.272
let's not have any at all.

00:09:14.404 --> 00:09:16.243
Hey, this is new.

00:09:16.267 --> 00:09:19.517
Did we just invent
preventive self-censorship?

00:09:21.166 --> 00:09:23.677
I think this is bigger than cartoons.

00:09:23.701 --> 00:09:26.323
This is about opinion and journalism.

00:09:26.347 --> 00:09:28.672
This, in the end, is about democracy.

00:09:30.872 --> 00:09:32.841
We now live in a world

00:09:32.865 --> 00:09:36.260
where moralistic mobs
gather on social media

00:09:36.284 --> 00:09:38.145
and rise like a storm.

00:09:38.724 --> 00:09:42.199
The most outraged voices
tend to define the conversation,

00:09:42.223 --> 00:09:44.468
and the angry crowd follows in.

00:09:45.195 --> 00:09:47.289
These social media mobs,

00:09:47.313 --> 00:09:50.183
sometimes fueled by interest groups,

00:09:50.207 --> 00:09:53.205
fall upon newsrooms
in an overwhelming blow.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:58.318
They send publishers and editors
scrambling for countermeasures.

00:09:58.342 --> 00:10:02.609
This leaves no room
for meaningful discussions.

00:10:02.633 --> 00:10:06.742
Twitter is a place for fury,
not for debate.

00:10:09.213 --> 00:10:10.459
And you know what?

00:10:10.483 --> 00:10:14.919
Someone described pretty well
our human condition in this noisy age.

00:10:14.943 --> 00:10:16.169
You know who?

00:10:16.609 --> 00:10:19.207
Shakespeare, 400 years ago.

00:10:19.231 --> 00:10:23.118
["(Life is) a tale told by an idiot, full
of sound and fury, signifying nothing."]

00:10:23.142 --> 00:10:25.862
This speaks to me.
Shakespeare is still very relevant, no?

00:10:25.886 --> 00:10:27.424
But the world has changed a bit.

00:10:27.448 --> 00:10:28.601
[Too long!]

00:10:28.625 --> 00:10:30.161
(Laughter)

00:10:30.185 --> 00:10:31.342
It's true.

00:10:31.366 --> 00:10:36.158
(Applause)

00:10:37.198 --> 00:10:41.217
You know, social media is both
a blessing and a curse for cartoons.

00:10:41.685 --> 00:10:45.803
This is the era of the image,
so they get shared, they get viral,

00:10:45.827 --> 00:10:48.792
but that also makes them a prime target.

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:53.037
More than often, the real target
behind the cartoon

00:10:53.061 --> 00:10:55.305
is the media that published it.

00:10:55.329 --> 00:10:56.626
[Covering Iraq?
No, Trump!]

00:10:56.650 --> 00:11:00.280
That relationship between
traditional media and social media

00:11:00.304 --> 00:11:01.933
is a funny one.

00:11:01.957 --> 00:11:04.681
On one hand, you have
the time-consuming process

00:11:04.705 --> 00:11:07.285
of information, verification, curation.

00:11:07.309 --> 00:11:10.074
On the other hand,
it's an open buffet, frankly,

00:11:10.098 --> 00:11:13.119
for rumors, opinions, emotions,

00:11:13.143 --> 00:11:15.535
amplified by algorithms.

00:11:16.575 --> 00:11:22.506
Even quality newspapers mimic the codes
of social networks on their websites.

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:26.498
They highlight the 10 most read,
the 10 most shared stories.

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:31.473
They should put forward
the 10 most important stories.

00:11:31.497 --> 00:11:38.227
(Applause)

00:11:40.166 --> 00:11:44.542
The media must not be
intimidated by social media,

00:11:44.566 --> 00:11:49.748
and editors should stop
being afraid of the angry mob.

00:11:49.772 --> 00:11:55.994
(Applause)

00:11:57.007 --> 00:12:00.709
We're not going to put up warnings
the way we do on cigarette packs, are we?

00:12:00.733 --> 00:12:02.274
[Satire can hurt your feelings]

00:12:02.298 --> 00:12:03.351
(Laughter)

00:12:03.375 --> 00:12:04.539
Come on.

00:12:04.563 --> 00:12:06.972
[Under your burkini
you could be hiding a sex bomb]

00:12:06.986 --> 00:12:10.752
Political cartoons are meant
to provoke, just like opinions.

00:12:10.776 --> 00:12:14.746
But before all, they are meant
to be thought-provoking.

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:16.034
You feel hurt?

00:12:16.058 --> 00:12:17.526
Just let it go.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:19.360
You don't like it?

00:12:19.384 --> 00:12:20.743
Look the other way.

00:12:22.459 --> 00:12:26.332
Freedom of expression
is not incompatible with dialogue

00:12:26.356 --> 00:12:27.931
and listening to each other.

00:12:27.955 --> 00:12:31.367
But it is incompatible with intolerance.

00:12:31.911 --> 00:12:36.942
(Applause)

00:12:37.897 --> 00:12:44.679
Let us not become our own censors
in the name of political correctness.

00:12:44.703 --> 00:12:47.284
We need to stand up, we need to push back,

00:12:47.308 --> 00:12:49.849
because if we don't,
we will wake up tomorrow

00:12:49.873 --> 00:12:51.504
in a sanitized world,

00:12:51.528 --> 00:12:55.712
where any form of satire and political
cartooning becomes impossible.

00:12:56.378 --> 00:13:01.966
Because, when political pressure
meets political correctness,

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:04.427
freedom of speech perishes.

00:13:05.089 --> 00:13:09.094
(Applause)

00:13:09.451 --> 00:13:12.904
Do you remember January 2015?

00:13:14.109 --> 00:13:19.130
With the massacre
of journalists and cartoonists

00:13:19.154 --> 00:13:20.971
at "Charlie Hebdo" in Paris,

00:13:20.995 --> 00:13:25.909
we discovered the most
extreme form of censorship:

00:13:26.822 --> 00:13:28.344
murder.

00:13:28.368 --> 00:13:29.757
Remember how it felt.

00:13:29.781 --> 00:13:31.288
[Without humor we are all dead]

00:13:31.312 --> 00:13:34.095
Whatever one thought
of that satirical magazine,

00:13:34.119 --> 00:13:37.988
however one felt about
those particular cartoons,

00:13:38.012 --> 00:13:42.650
we all sensed that something
fundamental was at stake,

00:13:42.674 --> 00:13:47.417
that citizens of free societies --
actually, citizens of any society --

00:13:47.441 --> 00:13:51.101
need humor as much as the air we breathe.

00:13:51.799 --> 00:13:54.138
This is why the extremists,

00:13:54.162 --> 00:13:59.702
the dictators, the autocrats and, frankly,
all the ideologues of the world

00:13:59.726 --> 00:14:01.621
cannot stand humor.

00:14:04.024 --> 00:14:07.373
In the insane world we live in right now,

00:14:07.397 --> 00:14:10.228
we need political cartoons more than ever.

00:14:11.474 --> 00:14:12.878
And we need humor.

00:14:14.425 --> 00:14:15.733
Thank you.

00:14:15.757 --> 00:14:22.725
(Applause)

